Here is some information about the Minix filesystem support in this version
of Mint.

**** Version 0.33

	Firstly my appologies for the mess in the original version. It crashed
on partitions with sectors > 512 bytes , I don't have any so this slipped 
through . The current version shouldn't  crash no matter what the sector size ,
it will however only support 512 byte and 1K sectors for minix partitions, to
support anything else is rather inefficient and messy , though I will add code
for larger 'illogical sectors' if there is sufficient demand. 
	There were a few other bugs in the original version , nothing of the 
trash-partition variety (at least none *I* found !) , but many things which 
either did not work or did not work properly. TOSIFY is a bit of a mess , 
I personally have it set but it can cause problems when running MiNT domain 
programs under TOS domain ones (unrecognised filenames messed up cwd etc) . 
There is no easy fix for this.Running TOS domain under MiNT domain , TOS under
TOS and MiNT under MiNT should be OK though .
	The i/o code has been overhauled somewhat to read/write consecutive 
sectors where possible ; this should speed things up considerably . Also 
sparse file support has been added (though not extensively tested so use it 
with care). 

NB With MiNT domain and TOS domain programs (with TOSIFY off) the name field
of a dta will *not* be null terminated,for 14 character filenames,this *must*
be allowed for.

**** Version 0.31

	I am well aware that the code may not look too pretty ! It is in the
process of being cleaned up at present.Since this could take a while I thought
it best to release a version that worked,but was ugly,rather than a version that
was functionally identical but looked nicer (and it gives me a chance to add new
features , and fix bugs,as well as tidying up the code in the next release).
	I have compiled this under gcc, I haven't tried to compile it under any
other compiler.It may well cause problems on a non-ANSI compiler.
	The code was written independently of the various Minix filesystems
about although it was impossible not to use some of the structures present in 
Minix (or ones similar to them). 
	The current version scans all GEM partitions when MiNT starts up, 
when I add floppy disk support it will probably do something different .
	You must  have Minix  stored under  a GEM partition , that is  one
that has the partition id 'GEM' and hasn't had its boot-sector obliterated ...
basically if a minix partition has an icon on the desktop which shows '0 bytes
in 0 items' you should be OK . 
	A Minix filesystem behaves more or less like a TOS one so you should be
able to access files on Minix partitions directly through other programs and add
TOS programs to Minix partitions (I have TeX running quite happily on one of my
partitions).
	As  stated  above  this version of  does not support  floppy disks , 
future versions will,just as soon as I work out a 'clean' way of handling it. 
Removable media on hard-drives is a non-starter as well.
	Filenames are converted to upper case and,although you'll get away
with most filenames,ones containing slashes will confuse 'minixfs'. [Note also
that filenames are truncated to avoid things like the desktop blowing up,this
will occasionally mean that the same filename appears twice in a directory.
][ This translation is no longer done by default -- ERS ].
	MiNT domain programs get the 14 characters of the Minix file
name in the 'dta' structure of Fsfirst/Fsnext with no translation . It is 
also permissible to open directories on a Minix filesystem unlike TOS and
deletion of open files is permissible too (but not advisable,unless you
can guarantee that it is a minix partition you are doing this on,as TOS
doesn't like this at all,and can trash a partition this way).
	No access checking is done so everyone is basically 'root' although
files/directories do have the uid/gid of the creator. I hope to remedy this
on later versions. You can only access regular files and directories at 
present,  so special files although they appear in 'dta' structures can't 
be opened or deleted . Sparse files will also cause trouble ... this is also
in the 'things to do' list. Fattrib is also a bit kludged and so setting files
to 'read-only' doesn't do a thing at present.
	There are assumptions that zone-size==block-size all over the code , 
since the current (and previous) versions of 'mkfs' wont allow anything else
this is valid ... for the moment , if this changes I'll fix it, at present 
minixfs just complains and wont access such a partition.

NOTE:	Since 'minixfs' does some low level things to a hard drive I cannot
guarantee it wont do something nasty ... so keep backups handy. Having said
that I haven't managed to do anything to my Minix partitions which even makes
'fsck' (under Minix)  complain  let alone trash a  partition  (If anyone
finds a repeatable way to get 'minixfs' to cause fsck to complain please tell
me and I'll fix it).

	Have fun ! Stephen N Henson. 

P.S. 'Minixfs' is not my number one priority at present so don't expect things
to be done overnight !

